---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import InstallBar from '../components/InstallBar.astro';
import Folder from '../components/Folder.astro';
import Sheet from '../components/Sheet.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import Slider from '../components/Slider.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
// shared.css first: the build links the sheets in the order a page first
// imports them, and puts style.css and docs.css in one file, so this is
// the order in dev and in production alike. style.css is layered (it
// loses to both); docs.css comes after shared.css and wins.
import '../marketing/shared.css';
import '../marketing/style.css';
import '../marketing/docs.css';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<Page
  title="Tour · Design Book"
  description="Every rule in Design Book with a live example: references, selectors that pick from a palette, readable colors, scales, inheritance and the renderers."
  fonts={['https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Valley+Sans:ital,wght@0,100..900;1,100..900&display=swap']}
>

  <section class="hero first-screen split split--wide-start">
    <Logo class="intro-logo" thin />
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>A tour of Design Book</h1>
      <p class="deck">Most design systems store answers: a hex picked here, a spacing
        chosen there. I kept wanting to store <em>how the answer was picked</em>
        instead: the highest-contrast text on a surface, the next bigger gap in
        a scale, the most vivid color that <em>still passes WCAG</em>. Change an
        input and every token that depends on it is worked out again, contrast
        included.</p>

      <p class="install-note">TypeScript · ESM</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-illustration" id="hero-illustration">
      <svg viewBox="-200 -200 400 400" aria-hidden="true">
        <g class="hi-ring" id="hi-ring"></g>
      </svg>
      <color-input class="hi-center" id="hi-center" value="#0d0a03" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input>
      <div class="hi-pill">
        <select class="hi-fn" id="hi-fn" aria-label="Selector function">
          <option value="bestContrastWith">bestContrastWith</option>
          <option value="minContrastWith">minContrastWith</option>
          <option value="closest">closest</option>
          <option value="furthestFrom">furthestFrom</option>
          <option value="mostVivid">mostVivid</option>
          <option value="leastVivid">leastVivid</option>
          <option value="lightest">lightest</option>
          <option value="darkest">darkest</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── The graph: hero visual ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="graph split" aria-labelledby="graph-title">
    <h2 class="rule-title" id="graph-title">A web of decisions, not a list of values.</h2>
    <div class="graph-text">
      <p>A book keeps its tokens in <em>scopes</em>, named groups like
        <code>brand</code> or <code>ui</code>; <code>brand.primary</code> is the
        token <code>primary</code> in <code>brand</code>.</p>
      <p>Each token is a node and each reference an edge. The library tracks
        references, computed values and selectors that read a whole scope, so
        when an input changes it recomputes exactly what depends on it, either
        right away or all at once with <code>flush()</code> in batch mode. A
        change that would create a cycle is refused, and
        <code>watch(key, fn)</code> calls you back when a value changes.</p>
      <p>The diagram on the right is a small demo book: a brand color, a ramp
        made from it, a spacing scale and the UI decisions on top, drawn by
        the built-in <code>SVGRenderer</code>. Change the brand color or flip
        the ramp and watch it redraw.</p>
      <div class="graph-controls">
        <label>
          <span>brand</span>
          <color-input id="graph-brand" value="#de5331" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input>
        </label>
        <button type="button" id="graph-invert">Flip ramp</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="graph-viz blueprint">
      <div id="graph-viz" aria-label="Dependency graph"></div>
      <!-- A card built from the book's ui tokens, painted by script.js. -->
      <div class="ui-card graph-card" id="graph-card" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="ui-card-line ui-card-line--title"></span>
        <span class="ui-card-line"></span>
        <span class="ui-card-line ui-card-line--short"></span>
        <span class="ui-card-button"><span class="ui-card-line"></span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── ref(): point at a source instead of copying it ───────────── -->
  <section class="ref-feature" aria-labelledby="ref-title">
    <div class="ref-feature-body split">
      <h2 class="rule-title" id="ref-title">References instead of copies.</h2>
      <div class="ref-feature-text">
        <p>This part you already know from Figma variables and CSS custom
          properties. A hex code pasted in two places is two decisions, and
          sooner or later they drift apart; <code>ref()</code> stores where a
          value comes from, the key and not the value, so changing the source
          changes everything that points at it. Refs chain and cross scopes,
          and in CSS the link stays a <code>var()</code>.</p>
        <p>It is the foundation, not the point. A reference can only say
          <em>use that one</em>; everything after this section is about the
          tokens that decide which one.</p>
      </div>

      <Folder>
        <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>brand.set('primary',
  color('#3c61a0'));

ui.set('link',
  ref('brand.primary'));
ui.set('focus', ref('ui.link'));
ui.set('border', ref('ui.link'));</Code></Sheet>
        <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
          <figure class="ref-stage">
            <div class="extends-pane">
              <div class="extends-rows extends-rows--keys" id="ref-rows"></div>
            </div>
            <pre is:raw class="code ref-css" id="ref-css" aria-label="CSS output"></pre>
          </figure>
        </Sheet>
      </Folder>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Why: what CSS cannot do ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="ref-feature" aria-labelledby="why-title">
    <div class="ref-feature-body split">
      <h2 class="rule-title" id="why-title">The reason is the token.</h2>
      <div class="ref-feature-text">
        <p>A custom property can point at another, and CSS's
          <code>contrast-color()</code> picks black or white. But CSS cannot
          choose from <em>your</em> palette by <em>your</em> rule. In Design
          Book the rule is the token: it documents the decision and makes it
          again whenever an input changes.</p>
        <p>On the right the surface never sits still. The text, the loudest ink
          that still reads, picks again every time.</p>
        <p>Rules come in two kinds. <a href="#transforms-title">Transforms</a>
          change a value, like <code>color-mix()</code> and <code>calc()</code>,
          only more. <a href="#kinds-title">Selectors</a> route: they pick one
          member of a scope, like <code>mostVivid</code> here.</p>
      </div>

      <Folder>
        <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>const ink = book.addScope('ink');
ink.set('black',  color('#0d0a03'));
ink.set('paper',  color('#fffaed'));
ink.set('blue',   color('#172c4f'));
ink.set('red',    color('#de5331'));
ink.set('yellow', color('#dab43d'));
ink.set('green',  color('#479549'));

// the reason, not the answer: the most
// vivid ink that still reads (4.5:1)
ui.set('text', mostVivid(ref('ink'), {
  readableOn: ref('ui.surface'),
}));

// set ui.surface to anything:
// the text decides again</Code></Sheet>
        <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
          <figure class="why-stage">
            <div class="why-sample" id="why-sample" aria-hidden="true">Read me.</div>
            <div class="extends-pane">
              <div class="extends-rows extends-rows--keys" id="why-rows"></div>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </Sheet>
      </Folder>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Selector functions: one panel, a switcher ─────────────────── -->
  <main class="sections">

    <header class="sections-head">
      <h2 class="rule-title" id="kinds-title">Kinds of decision</h2>
      <p>A selector takes a scope of candidates and returns the member that
        fits its rule, and picks again whenever a member changes, is added or
        is removed. These come built in.</p>
    </header>

    <nav class="fn-index" aria-label="Selectors">
      <div class="fn-index-group">
        <p class="fn-index-head" data-ledger-head>Compare</p>
        <ul data-ledger>
          <li><a href="#closest">closest</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next-step">nextLarger · nextSmaller</a></li>
          <li><a href="#furthest">furthestFrom</a></li>
          <li><a href="#lightness">lightest · darkest</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="fn-index-group">
        <p class="fn-index-head" data-ledger-head>Contrast</p>
        <ul data-ledger>
          <li><a href="#best-contrast">bestContrastWith</a></li>
          <li><a href="#min-contrast">minContrastWith</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="fn-index-group">
        <p class="fn-index-head" data-ledger-head>Color only</p>
        <ul data-ledger>
          <li><a href="#vivid">mostVivid · leastVivid</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="fn-index-group">
        <p class="fn-index-head" data-ledger-head>Position</p>
        <ul data-ledger>
          <li><a href="#nth">nth</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sibling">sibling</a></li>
          <li><a href="#random">random</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="fn-panels">

    <!-- bestContrastWith -->
    <article class="fn" id="best-contrast">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">bestContrastWith</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">bestContrastWith( target, scope, { direction?, readableOn?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> WCAG ratio</li><li class="kind-size"><b>size</b> value ratio</li><li class="kind-text"><b>text style</b> font-size ratio</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Change either ink</em> and every contrast is checked again.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">The member with the highest contrast against a target: the WCAG ratio for colors, the ratio between two values for sizes and text styles, so a heading can be the style that stands out most from the body. The check is part of the rule, so you don't redo it after every redesign.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>const ink = book.addScope('ink');
ink.set('a', color('#172c4f'));
ink.set('b', color('#f3d36b'));

// on every surface: whichever ink reads best
ui.set('label', bestContrastWith(
  ref('ui.surface'), ref('ink'),
))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="contrast-row" id="demo-contrast"></div>
              <div class="stub contrast-inks">
                <label><color-input id="contrast-ink-a" value="#172c4f" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input><span>ink.a</span></label>
                <label><color-input id="contrast-ink-b" value="#f3d36b" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input><span>ink.b</span></label>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- closest -->
    <article class="fn" id="closest">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">closest</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">closest( target, scope, { direction?, upTo?, minDistance?, readableOn?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> OKLab distance</li><li class="kind-size"><b>size</b> value</li><li class="kind-text"><b>text style</b> font size</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Work freely,</em> land on your system.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">Pick a color by eye, take a size from a mockup, pull a brand color out of a partner's logo: <code>closest</code> snaps it to the nearest value your design system actually has, so the freedom never leaks a one-off into the system. Colors are compared by distance in OKLab, so colors that look alike are treated alike; sizes and text styles by value, so the 13px from the mockup lands on your 12px.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>const space = book.addScope('space');
space.set('xs', px(4));   space.set('s', px(8));
space.set('m', px(12));   space.set('l', px(16));
space.set('xl', px(24));  space.set('2xl', px(32));
space.set('3xl', px(48));

// any value: a guess, a mockup, a slider
ui.set('gap', closest(px(13), ref('space')));

// any color: picked by eye, out of a logo
ui.set('accent', closest(color('#7f4dc4'), ref('palette')));</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Colors" class="closest-colors">
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="closest-swatches" id="closest-swatches" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              <p class="closest-readout" id="closest-color-readout"></p>
              <div class="stub">
                <label class="closest-pick">
                  <color-input id="closest-color" value="#7f4dc4" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input>
                  <span>any color</span>
                </label>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Sizes" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="closest-stage">
                <div class="closest-ruler" id="closest-ruler" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <p class="closest-readout" id="closest-readout"></p>
              </div>
              <div class="stub closest-free">
                <Slider id="closest-value" min="0" max="56" step="1" value="13" aria-label="Any value, in px" />
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- furthestFrom -->
    <article class="fn" id="furthest">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">furthestFrom</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">furthestFrom( target, scope, { maxDistance?, readableOn?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> OKLab distance</li><li class="kind-size"><b>size</b> value</li><li class="kind-text"><b>text style</b> font size</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Furthest from</em> what is already there.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">The mirror of <code>closest</code>: the member that looks least like the target. An accent that stands apart from the surface, a display size far from the body text. Colors by OKLab distance, sizes and text styles by value. <code>maxDistance</code> keeps it in reach: the furthest member that is no further than that.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>// the palette color that looks least like the target
ui.set('accent', furthestFrom(
  ref('ui.surface'),
  ref('palette'),
))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="ffr-ruler" id="ffr-ruler" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              <p class="ffr-readout" id="ffr-readout"></p>
              <div class="stub ffr-stub">
                <label><color-input id="ffr-target" value="#de5331" initial-colorspace="hex" no-alpha=""></color-input><span>target</span></label>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- minContrastWith -->
    <article class="fn" id="min-contrast">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">minContrastWith</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">minContrastWith( target, scope, { minContrast?, readableOn?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> WCAG ratio</li><li class="kind-size"><b>size</b> value ratio</li><li class="kind-text"><b>text style</b> font-size ratio</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>As subtle as possible</em> while still readable.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">The lowest contrast that still reaches a ratio. For colors that is WCAG (4.5 by default): hairlines, captions and dim labels that pass the next audit without anyone checking them by hand. It works the other way too: a hairline, a divider or a ruled background at a ratio around 1.2 stays faint on any surface but never disappears. For sizes and text styles it is the ratio between two values: the smallest heading that still reads as bigger than the body.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>// text that just passes AA
ui.set('caption', minContrastWith(
  ref('ui.surface'), neutral,
  { minContrast: 4.5 },
));

// a line that is faint, but always there
ui.set('hairline', minContrastWith(
  ref('ui.surface'), neutral,
  { minContrast: 1.2 },
));

// the smallest heading that still reads as bigger
ui.set('heading', minContrastWith(
  ref('type.body'), ref('type'),
  { minContrast: 1.25, direction: 'larger' },
));</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Sizes" class="min-sizes">
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="ms-stage">
                <div class="ms-scale" id="ms-scale" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <div class="ms-sample">
                  <p class="ms-heading" id="ms-heading">Field notes</p>
                  <p class="ms-body">Twelve walks along the river, with maps. The body is 16px.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="ms-readout" id="ms-readout"></p>
              <div class="stub ms-stub">
                <span>minContrast</span>
                <Slider id="ms-ratio" min="1.05" max="3" step="0.05" value="1.25" aria-label="minContrast" />
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Colors" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="min-stage">
                <div class="min-card">
                  <div class="min-label">ratio 1.2 · hairline</div>
                  <div class="min-sample min-sample--lines" id="min-sample-12"><span class="min-text"></span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="min-card">
                  <div class="min-label">ratio 3 · AA large</div>
                  <div class="min-sample" id="min-sample-3"><span class="min-text"></span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="min-card">
                  <div class="min-label">ratio 4.5 · AA</div>
                  <div class="min-sample" id="min-sample-45"><span class="min-text"></span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="min-card">
                  <div class="min-label">ratio 7 · AAA</div>
                  <div class="min-sample" id="min-sample-7"><span class="min-text"></span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- nextLarger / nextSmaller -->
    <article class="fn" id="next-step">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">nextLarger · nextSmaller</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">nextLarger|nextSmaller( target, scope, { minDistance?, not? } ) = closest( …, { direction } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-size"><b>size</b> value</li><li class="kind-text"><b>text style</b> font size</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Spacing and type that know their scale.</em></p>
          <p class="fn-detail">Steps to the next member up or down, in px, rem, ms or whatever unit the scale uses, or to the next text style by its font size. Add a step to the scale and every gap, padding, duration and heading that referred to it moves along.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>ui.set('gap', nextLarger(
  ref('space.m'),
  space,
  { minDistance: 6 },
))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="step-stage">
                <div class="step-scale" id="step-scale" aria-label="Spacing scale"></div>
                <div class="step-readout">
                  <div class="step-cell">
                    <span class="step-key">nextSmaller</span>
                    <span class="step-result" id="step-smaller">—</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="step-cell">
                    <span class="step-key">target</span>
                    <span class="step-result" id="step-target-out">12px</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="step-cell">
                    <span class="step-key">nextLarger</span>
                    <span class="step-result" id="step-larger">—</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-controls stub">
                <label>
                  <span class="step-lbl">target</span>
                  <Slider id="step-target" min="0" max="6" step="1" value="3" />
                  <span class="step-val" id="step-target-val">12px</span>
                </label>
                <label>
                  <span class="step-lbl">minDistance</span>
                  <Slider id="step-min" min="0" max="20" step="1" value="0" />
                  <span class="step-val" id="step-min-val">0px</span>
                </label>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- mostVivid / leastVivid -->
    <article class="fn" id="vivid">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">mostVivid · leastVivid</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">mostVivid|leastVivid( scope, { lightness?, readableOn?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> OKLCH chroma</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Your accent is probably already in your palette.</em></p>
          <p class="fn-detail">The two ends of the chroma axis. <code>mostVivid</code> finds the loudest member, usually the accent, which is handy when the set is generated and you can't know in advance which step that will be. <code>leastVivid</code> finds the most neutral one among your brand colors. Near black and white no color carries much chroma, so <code>lightness: [min, max]</code> compares only the members in the band you mean. Both can also be limited to colors that read on a given surface.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>ui.set('accent',  mostVivid(brand))
ui.set('surface', leastVivid(brand))

// only among the mid tones
ui.set('accent', mostVivid(brand, {
  lightness: [0.4, 0.8],
}))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="vivid-stage" id="vivid-stage"></div>
              <div class="stub vivid-stub">
                <span class="vivid-lbl">lightness</span>
                <div class="range2" id="vivid-range">
                  <span class="range2-band" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  <Slider id="vivid-lo" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0" aria-label="lightness, min" />
                  <Slider id="vivid-hi" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="1" aria-label="lightness, max" />
                </div>
                <span class="vivid-val" id="vivid-val">0–1</span>
                <button type="button" class="stub-button" id="vivid-new">New random palette</button>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- lightest / darkest -->
    <article class="fn" id="lightness">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">lightest · darkest</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">lightest|darkest( scope, { readableOn?, readableContrast?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-color"><b>color</b> OKLCH lightness</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>The ends of the scale,</em> wherever they end up.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">The members with the highest and lowest OKLCH lightness. Make your surface <code>lightest</code> and your ink <code>darkest</code>, and they still land on the right colors after the palette is generated again. Add <code>readableOn</code> to only consider members that read on that surface.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>ui.set('surface', lightest(brand))
ui.set('ink',     darkest(brand))

// the lightest member that still reads on the page
ui.set('soft-ink', lightest(brand, {
  readableOn: ref('ui.surface'),
}))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="lightness-stage" id="lightness-stage"></div>
              <div class="stub lightness-stub">
                <label class="lightness-toggle">
                  <input type="checkbox" id="lightness-readable" />
                  readableOn the page (4.5:1)
                </label>
                <button type="button" class="stub-button" id="lightness-new">New random palette</button>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- random -->
    <article class="fn" id="random">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">random</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">random( scope, { type, seed?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-any"><b>any</b> filtered by type</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Random,</em> but it stays put.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">Picks a member of a scope at random. Each token keeps its own seed, so the value stays the same across re-resolves and machines until you reshuffle on purpose: per release, per user or per environment. <code>type</code> limits the pool to colors, dimensions or strings.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>ui.set('accent',
  random(brand, { type: 'color' }))

ui.set('promo-bg', random(brand, {
  type: 'color',
  seed: 'spring-2026',
}))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Result" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="random-stage" id="random-stage"></div>
              <div class="stub random-stub">
                <label class="random-seed-lbl">
                  seed
                  <input id="random-seed" class="random-seed-input" type="text" value="spring-2026" spellcheck="false">
                </label>
                <button id="random-shuffle" class="stub-button" type="button">New seed</button>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- nth -->
    <article class="fn" id="nth">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">nth</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">nth( scope, index, { not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-any"><b>any</b> by position</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>Pick by position</em> instead of by name.</p>
          <p class="fn-detail">When a ramp or a scale generates your tokens, the order carries the meaning: lightest first, darkest last; smallest first, largest last. <code>nth</code> reads that order directly, which makes it a handy way to move along a color ramp: the surface at one end, the text at the other, the muted tone in the middle, whatever the ramp is regenerated to. Integers work like <code>Array.at()</code> (<code>-1</code> is the last member), and fractions between 0 and 1 pick a <em>relative</em> position, so <code>0.5</code> is the midtone however many stops there are. (<code>1.0</code> counts as the integer 1; use <code>-1</code> for the last member.)</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>// a scale generated smallest → largest
ui.set('hairline', nth(ref('space'), 0))   // smallest
ui.set('section',  nth(ref('space'), -1))  // largest
ui.set('gap',      nth(ref('space'), 0.5)) // the middle

// light and dark: the same rules, the ramp written
// the other way round
const steps = mode === 'dark' ? [...ramp].reverse() : ramp;
steps.forEach((c, i) => scale.set(`${i}`, color(c)));
ui.set('bg',     nth(ref('scale'), 0));
ui.set('text',   nth(ref('scale'), -1));
ui.set('muted',  nth(ref('scale'), 0.6));
ui.set('border', nth(ref('scale'), 0.15));</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Modes" class="nth-modes">
            <figure class="demo">
              <div class="nm-ramp" id="nm-ramp" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              <div class="nm-card" id="nm-card">
                <p class="nm-title">Field notes</p>
                <p class="nm-muted">Twelve walks along the river</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stub">
                <div class="switch nm-switch" id="nm-switch">
                  <button type="button" data-mode="light" aria-pressed="true">light</button>
                  <button type="button" data-mode="dark" aria-pressed="false">dark</button>
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Sizes" front>
            <figure class="demo">
              <table class="ledger nth-table">
                <thead><tr><th>index</th><th>from end</th><th>key</th><th>value</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody id="nth-rows"></tbody>
              </table>
              <p class="nth-readout" id="nth-readout"></p>
              <div class="stub nth-stub">
                <span>index</span>
                <Slider id="nth-index" min="0" max="1" step="0.05" value="0.5" aria-label="index" />
              </div>
            </figure>
          </Sheet>
        </Folder>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- sibling -->
    <article class="fn" id="sibling">
      <header class="fn-head">
        <h3 class="rule-title">sibling</h3>
        <code is:raw class="sig">sibling( ref, scope?, steps?, { toward?, wrap?, not? } )</code>
        <ul class="fn-kinds" aria-label="Works on"><li class="kind-any"><b>any</b> along the scale it comes from</li></ul>
      </header>
      <div class="fn-body split">
        <div class="fn-pitch">
          <p class="fn-claim"><em>One step lighter than whatever the surface is.</em></p>
          <p class="fn-detail">Steps through a scale, relative to a token: <code>+1</code> is the next member, <code>-1</code> the previous one. Hover and pressed states follow the surface instead of hard-coding a second shade. The scale is the token's own scope, or the one you name (<code>ref('blue')</code>), or wherever <a href="#ref-source-title"><code>source</code></a> leads (more on that further down); it is never guessed. Steps stop at the ends, or go round with <code>wrap</code>; with <code>toward</code> a step goes in the direction of another member, so a hover steps toward the page, lighter on a light one and darker on a dark one.</p>
        </div>
        <Folder>
          <Sheet name="code" label="Code"><Code is:raw>// keys from scaleNames(9, 'creatures'):
// tardigrade, ant, beetle, … cat, … whale
ui.set('gap',       ref('space.cat'))
ui.set('gap-loose', sibling(ref('space.cat'), 1)) // wolf
ui.set('gap-tight', sibling(ref('space.cat'), -1)) // hamster

ui.set('surface',       ref('gray.g100'))
ui.set('surface-hover', sibling(ref('gray.g100'), 1))
ui.set('surface-press', sibling(ref('gray.g100'), 2))</Code></Sheet>
          <Sheet name="result" label="Colors" class="sibling-colors">
            <figure class="demo">
              <figcaption>Click a tile to move the anchor.</figcaption>
              <div class="sibling-stage" id="sibling-stage"></div>
              <div class="sibling-controls stub">
                <div class="sibling-offsets" id="sibling-offsets" role="group" aria-label="Offset">
